  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="74a88f37-794f-47ca-8826-0b457de51fd7"><problem><para>
      <para>In questions 1 - 5, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the system.</para>
      <para>1. \(3x + y &gt; 5\)</para>
      <para>    \(2x − y ≤ 10\) </para>
      <para>  a. (3,−3) </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Yes. (3, -3) is a solution to the system. </para>
      <para>When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
        3(3) + (-3) &gt; 5 &amp;   &amp;2(3) − (-3)≤10 \\
        6&gt;5 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 9≤10 \checkmark
        \end{array}\) </para>
      <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
